1. `SETS` - Store only unique elements
	 - `set<data_type> set_name` = {element1,element2...}; - for creating initialized set
	 - `set<data_type> set_name`; - Empty set
	 - `set_name.insert(5); `--> for inserting element in set and by this 5 will be a element of set_name.
	 - `set_name.size();` --> For number of elements in set.
	 - Set is already sorted in increasing order. 
	 - `*(set_name).rbegin()` --> will give maximum value of set.
	 - `*(set_name).begin()` --> will give minimum value of set.

2. `size_t` --> This is a non-negative integer storing data type , generally used for counting elements 
    or something related to counting and this handle any number of numbers in it based on your hardware 
    architecture but int has a limit of 2 billions numbers only then it will wrap around negative part.

	-  **`long long` works:** Yes, `long long` can handle up to $9 \times 10^{18}$ and won't overflow.
    
	- **Why not use it for sizes:** Standard library functions and containers (like `.size()`) are strictly 
      designed to return `size_t` by definition.
	
	- **Portability:** `size_t` matches the exact native word size of your CPU architecture (32-bit on 32-bit 
      systems, 64-bit on 64-bit systems), ensuring maximum performance and compatibility.
	
	- Whenever you store the value returned by `.size()`, always use `size_t`.

	- And ,using a descriptive name like `size_t count` or `size_t size` immediately tells anyone reading
    your code that the variable holds a count or a size.
	
	-  Also use size_t for loop iterator integer defining for(size_t i = 0; i < condition ; i++){body}
	
		- **Never Negative:** Containers can't have negative sizes, so using an unsigned type like `size_t` 
        prevents bugs with negative loop bounds.
    
		- **Architecture Match:** It matches your CPU's native word size for optimal speed.
    
		- **No Warnings:** Comparing an `int` loop counter to `.size()` (which returns `size_t`) causes 
        compiler warnings; matching types eliminates them.
